import {texEq as eq} from './math.js';
import {section,table,cite} from './topic-tools.js';
const report=()=>cite('https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15763','GLM-5 report');
const scrollTable=(caption,heads,rows)=>table(caption,heads,rows).replace('<div class="dense-table-wrap">','<div class="dense-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable GLM-5 comparison">');
export function glmRecipeDetails(link){return `
${section('glm-block','GLM-5: trace two independent sparse paths',`<p>A decoder block first applies pre-RMSNorm and attention, then adds the residual. A second pre-RMSNorm prepares the FFN, followed by another residual addition. Blocks 0–2 use dense SwiGLU with intermediate width 12,288. Blocks 3–77 use MoE. Every block uses MLA with sparse token selection. Attention sparsity selects past positions; MoE sparsity selects feed-forward networks.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}x\in\mathbb{R}^{6144}&\longrightarrow c^Q\in\mathbb{R}^{2048}\longrightarrow 64\times 256\ \text{query features}\\x&\longrightarrow [c^{KV}\in\mathbb{R}^{512};\ k^R\in\mathbb{R}^{64}]\end{aligned}`,'Shapes are per token. Query compression passes through RMSNorm before expansion. The 512-feature KV latent is normalized before expansion into 192 non-rotary key and 256 value features per head.')}
<p>The separate 64-feature rotary key is shared across heads. Each complete query/key head joins 192 non-rotary features and 64 rotary features. Interleaved RoPE rotates only the final slice, with base 1,000,000 and the default configured rule. Sixty-four configured KV heads do not imply ordinary independent full-width KV projections.</p>
<div class="dense-flow" role="img" aria-label="GLM-5 indexer: query latent to 32 heads; hidden state to one normalized key; ReLU and learned head mixing; causal top 2048; main MLA reads selected raw KV"><div>Query latent 2,048<br>32 × 128 queries</div><span>↘</span><div>ReLU scores<br>Learned head mixture</div><span>→</span><div>Causal mask<br>Top 2,048 positions</div><span>→</span><div>Main MLA<br>Original KV entries</div></div>
<p>The key branch maps the width-6,144 hidden state to one width-128 indexer key, then applies LayerNorm. Indexer heads contain 64 rotary and 64 pass-through features. A learned token-dependent scalar mixture combines the 32 rectified head scores before causal top-k selection. Selected positions cannot include future tokens.</p>
<p>Main value aggregation scales with the selected-key budget, but the smaller indexer still scans all causal pairs. The reported 1.5–2× reduction in long-sequence attention compute follows adaptation; it is not exact equivalence to dense attention. During RL, the team freezes the indexer and uses a deterministic selection path to avoid training/rollout mismatch. Naming <code>torch.topk</code> alone does not guarantee portable tie ordering across devices.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\mathrm{main\ aggregation}=O(Lk),\qquad\mathrm{indexer\ scan}=O(L^2),\qquad k=2048`,'L is sequence length and k is the maximum selected-key budget. The costs have different feature-width constants; this expression is not a complete layer FLOP or latency model.')}
${eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}P_e&=3\cdot 6144\cdot 2048=37{,}748{,}736\\P_{\mathrm{stored,experts}}&=75\cdot 257\cdot P_e\approx 727.61\times 10^9\\P_{\mathrm{active,experts}}&=75\cdot 9\cdot P_e\approx 25.48\times 10^9\end{aligned}`,'Three matrices form each expert. One MoE stores 256 routed plus one shared expert, about 9.70B expert parameters. A token executes eight routed plus one shared. These counts exclude attention, dense blocks, routers and other state; they do not reconstruct the reported 744B/40B convention.')}
<p>The <code>noaux_tc</code> correction bias changes selection, while original sigmoid affinities determine normalized mixture weights scaled by 2.5. Final RMSNorm precedes an untied 154,880-way vocabulary head. The checkpoint sets BF16 dtype, RMSNorm epsilon 10⁻⁵, no attention bias, zero attention dropout and 202,752 maximum positions. Configured capacity is not a guarantee of retrieval quality. The release uses an MIT license.</p>
<p><a href="${link('models/attention')}">Attention →</a> and <a href="${link('models/moe')}">MoE →</a> own the general mechanisms. ${cite('https://huggingface.co/zai-org/GLM-5/blob/main/config.json','Checkpoint configuration')} · ${cite('https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/blob/main/src/transformers/models/glm_moe_dsa/modeling_glm_moe_dsa.py','MLA/DSA implementation')} · ${report()}</p>`)}
${section('glm-run','GLM-5: connect data, schedules and execution',`<p>Code and reasoning receive early emphasis. Learned web-quality and world-knowledge classifiers supplement mathematical/scientific filtering and language-specific classifiers for lower-resource languages. Natural long documents and synthetic long-range dependency tasks serve different context needs. Repository data joins files, commits, issues, pull requests and source state: the model can connect a reported problem to a changing repository and its repair, rather than see isolated code alone.</p>
${scrollTable('GLM-5 schedules belong to different training objectives',['Phase','Exposure / length','Learning-rate policy'],[['Main pretraining','About 27T; mixed lengths','Warm from 0 to 2×10⁻⁴; cosine to 4×10⁻⁵; batch-size warmup'],['Mid-training','1T at 32K; 500B at 128K; 50B near 200K','Linear 4×10⁻⁵→10⁻⁵'],['Indexer warm-up','1,000 steps; 14 sequences × 202,752; main model frozen','5×10⁻³→2×10⁻⁴'],['Joint sparse adaptation','20B tokens; backbone and indexer train together','Constant 10⁻⁵']])}
<p>Muon Split orthogonalizes MLA Q/K/V up-projections independently by head. The reported comparison matches GQA-8 while keeping attention logits stable without clipping. This partitions update geometry, not merely a scalar learning rate. <a href="${link('training/optimization')}#heads">Head-wise optimization →</a></p>
<p>Interleaved pipelines can place the MTP module flexibly while sharing the main output head. Pipeline-aware ZeRO-2 uses rolling full-gradient buffers. Communication avoids redundant Muon synchronization. Layer-granular CPU activation offload, selective recomputation and sequence-chunked vocabulary loss reduce different memory terms; deferred weight gradients alter scheduling.</p>
<p>Workload-aware sequence reordering, dynamic attention redistribution and flexible context groups reduce imbalance. Hierarchical expert all-to-all handles routed-token exchange. These are execution dependencies, not evidence that one generic parallel mesh reproduces the run. <a href="${link('training/parallelism')}">Distributed Training →</a></p>
<p>SFT uses up to 202,752 context positions and executable agent trajectories generated with expert models and rejection sampling. Reasoning can be emitted before replies or tools, retained across turns, and controlled per turn. Recoverable errors can remain in the prefix with their loss masked. Masking prevents imitation of the mistake while allowing supervision of its correction.</p>
<p>The asynchronous agent learner records behavior probabilities with exact sampled token IDs. It uses double-sided importance treatment, policy-age and invalid-run filters. Incomplete groups are padded only when enough valid members survive; otherwise the group is dropped. Missing outcomes are not genuine task failures. Periodic weight synchronization and optimizer reset after rollout-engine updates are part of the operational recipe.</p>
<p>Rollout-ID-aware cache hashing, FP8 inference, MTP and prefill/decode separation support more than 1,000 concurrent trajectories. Environment tasks span Python, Java, Go, C, C++, JavaScript, TypeScript, PHP and Ruby. Docker/Harbor terminal builds, the search graph and static/runtime/perceptual slide checks provide distinct feedback. <a href="${link('post-training/agents')}">Agent Training →</a> ${report()}</p>`)}
${section('glm-evidence','GLM-5: separate weights, draft steps and harness results',`<p>One parameterized MTP module shares weights across three training unroll positions. Four speculative steps yield reported average accepted length 2.76 versus 2.55 for DeepSeek-V3.2 in that setup. This is not a measured universal throughput multiplier. SFT includes INT4 quantization-aware training with a reported bitwise-identical offline quantization/training kernel; generic BF16 downloads do not automatically provide that path.</p>
${scrollTable('Selected GLM-5 report results; task and harness scope remain part of the score',['Benchmark / condition','Reported score'],[['HLE','30.5'],['HLE with tools','50.4'],['AIME 2026 I','92.7'],['GPQA-Diamond','86.0'],['LongBench v2','64.5'],['SWE-bench Verified','77.8'],['SWE-bench Multilingual','73.3'],['Terminal-Bench 2, Terminus','56.2; 60.7 on verified subset'],['BrowseComp','62.0; 75.9 with context management'],['tau2','89.7'],['MCP Atlas','67.8']])}
<p>CCBenchV2 gives a less uniform picture: 42.1±1.21 resolved on SWE-rebench January 2026,52.3 on chained tasks and 25.8 pass@1 on the backend benchmark. These measures are not interchangeable. A strong open-weight result does not mean every proprietary reference loses on every task.</p>
<p>BrowseComp starts at 55.3. Keeping the latest five interaction rounds and folding older tool observations raises it to 62.0. Adding a discard-all rule beyond 32K raises it to 75.9. The weights did not become a new architecture between these context-policy settings. Tailored prompts, custom harnesses, repaired subsets and model judges constrain cross-model interpretation.</p>
<p>GLM-5.2 later extends configured context to 1,048,576 and introduces four-layer IndexShare from the 128K continued-training stage. Only integer selected-position lists are shared. Every layer retains its own queries, weights, values, output, residual update and MoE. A full indexer is not full dense attention.</p>
${scrollTable('GLM-5.2 coding ablation: seven MTP steps, cumulative changes',['Setup','Average accepted length'],[['Baseline','4.56'],['+IndexShare+KVShare','5.10'],['+rejection sampling','5.29'],['+end-to-end total-variation loss','5.47']])}
<p>KVShare retains target-produced KV across later draft steps instead of mixing target and draft states. The final gain is about 20%, but the cumulative table cannot isolate IndexShare. The reported 2.9× per-token FLOP reduction at 1M is not 2.9× throughput or proportional cache savings. <a href="${link('execution/speculation')}#recipes">Speculative Decoding →</a></p>
<p>The original recipe changes architecture, data, scale, post-training and harness together. Internal data and environments limit independent reproduction. Sparse computation still requires storing or distributing all expert weights. Keep these costs and empirical retrieval limits attached to the results. ${report()} · ${cite('https://huggingface.co/zai-org/GLM-5','Model card')} · ${cite('https://github.com/zai-org/GLM-5','Official repository')} · ${cite('https://huggingface.co/blog/zai-org/glm-52-blog','GLM-5.2 release')} · ${cite('https://sebastianraschka.com/blog/2026/glm-5-2-indexshare.html','IndexShare explanation')} · ${cite('https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12201','IndexCache')}</p>`)}
`;}
